From b3592b97e64e5da7ded6fb867f32299047177a1b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 07:51:37 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ElementsGeometry and heat transfer tests: review (nodes
 kernel, stale caches)

- The jacobians of all the meshes are computed by the kernel, from the
  coordinates of the nodes of the transformations of the elements: the
  nodes of the mesh if it has some (any order, ordered by nodes or by
  components, continuous or discontinuous as for the periodic meshes), its
  vertices otherwise, as `mfem::Mesh::GetElementTransformation`. The
  geometric factors of MFEM are no longer used: for the meshes with nodes,
  their cache was indexed by the address of the integration rule, and they
  assumed tensor product rules for the quadrangles and hexahedra (a rule
  whose points are not ordered as a tensor product gave jacobians wrong by
  57%).
- The values of the shape functions of the unknowns, cached by their
  finite element for the address of the integration rule, are checked
  against the number of points of the rule (computeElementsGeometry,
  computeUnknownsDerivatives): a rule destroyed and replaced by another
  with a different number of points at the same address is detected. The
  documentation states that the rule must outlive the finite element
  space.
- ElementsGeometryTest: curved meshes (hexahedra of order 2 ordered by
  nodes, tetrahedra and quadrangles of order 3 ordered by components), a
  periodic mesh (discontinuous nodes), each distorted, mirrored, with rules
  at the same address and a rule whose points are in the reverse order;
  checks of the cached shape functions and of their refusal.
- HeatTransferKernelsTest: the temperatures at the integration points are
  given to the behaviours with the EXTERNAL_STORAGE mode, so that they are
  really compared (the default mode copied them, and the comparison
  compared the initial values), and the reference temperatures must not be
  uniform; a temperature is imposed on z = 1 in 3D, so that the isotropic
  case depends on all the coordinates; the two materials case has two
  anisotropic materials with different material axes.

Mutations detected: strides of the nodes ordered by nodes and by
components, the two checks of the cached shape functions, z component of
the temperature gradient, copy of the temperature, storage mode of the
test.
---
 include/MFEMMGIS/Batch/ElementsGeometry.hxx |  40 ++--
 src/Batch/ElementsGeometry.cxx              | 196 ++++++++++++++------
 src/Batch/ElementsGeometryKernels.cxx       |  14 +-
 src/Batch/ElementsGeometryKernels.hxx       |  36 ++--
 src/Batch/Gradients.cxx                     |   9 +
 tests/ElementsGeometryTest.cxx              | 159 ++++++++++++++--
 tests/HeatTransferKernelsTest.cxx           |  58 +++---
 7 files changed, 383 insertions(+), 129 deletions(-)

diff --git a/include/MFEMMGIS/Batch/ElementsGeometry.hxx b/include/MFEMMGIS/Batch/ElementsGeometry.hxx
index 5ade59bdc2..02977028e1 100644
--- a/include/MFEMMGIS/Batch/ElementsGeometry.hxx
+++ b/include/MFEMMGIS/Batch/ElementsGeometry.hxx
@@ -72,23 +72,31 @@ namespace mfem_mgis::batch {
    * \brief computes the geometric data of all the elements of the mesh for
    * an integration rule
    *
-   * For a mesh without nodes, the jacobians are computed by a kernel from
-   * the vertices of the elements and the finite element of the
-   * transformations of MFEM (`mfem::Mesh::GetTransformationFEforElementType`):
-   * - `mfem::Mesh::GetGeometricFactors` would give nodes to the mesh
-   *   (`mfem::Mesh::EnsureNodes`), and the element transformations of all
-   *   the computations which follow would use them, which makes the
-   *   computations element by element about 50% slower;
-   * - computing the jacobians from a copy of the vertices in a finite
-   *   element space of order one costs between 0.7 s and 2.1 s for 1.6
-   *   million tetrahedra, depending on the mesh and on the device
-   *   (construction of the finite element space and projection of the
-   *   coordinates on the host), against about 10 ms for the kernel on the
-   *   GPU and 0.3 s on the host.
+   * The jacobians are computed by a kernel from the coordinates of the
+   * nodes of the transformations of the elements, as by
+   * `mfem::Mesh::GetElementTransformation`: the nodes of the mesh if it has
+   * some, its vertices otherwise (with the finite element of the
+   * transformations of MFEM, `mfem::Mesh::GetTransformationFEforElementType`).
+   * The gradients of the shape functions of the transformations are
+   * computed for the integration rule, without the caches of the finite
+   * elements of MFEM, indexed by the address of the rule. The geometric
+   * factors of MFEM are not used:
+   * - for a mesh without nodes, `mfem::Mesh::GetGeometricFactors` would give
+   *   nodes to the mesh (`mfem::Mesh::EnsureNodes`), and the element
+   *   transformations of all the computations which follow would use them,
+   *   which makes the computations element by element about 50% slower;
+   *   computing them from a copy of the vertices in a finite element space
+   *   of order one costs 0.7 s on the host and 2.1 s with the GPU enabled
+   *   for 1.6 million tetrahedra (construction of the finite element space
+   *   and projection of the coordinates on the host), against about 10 ms
+   *   for the kernel on the GPU and 0.3 s on the host;
+   * - for the quadrangles and hexahedra, they assume a tensor product
+   *   integration rule.
    *
-   * For a mesh with nodes, the jacobians are the geometric factors of MFEM,
-   * computed for this geometric data (the geometric factors of the mesh
-   * are destroyed if its nodes are modified).
+   * The values and gradients of the shape functions of the unknowns
+   * (`maps`) are the ones cached by the finite element of the unknowns: the
+   * integration rule must not be destroyed while the finite element space
+   * exists.
    *
    * \return true on success
    * \param[in, out] ctx: execution context
